post_theorem_intro_text:
The main advantage of Pintz's method is that we only need to consider the influence of zeroes of Dirichlet $L$-functions for a single moduli and  in a highly restricted area.   This feature has been shared with Linnik's problem on the least prime in an arithmetic progression. The best numerical result in this topic  is due to Xylouris \cite{Xylouris}, based on a refinement of  the method of Heath-Brown \cite{Heath-Brown}. In Pintz's argument, the proof of Theorem 1.1  can be  reduced to showing   the following result, which can be regarded as a two-dimensional version of Linnik's problem. 

 \begin{theorem}Let $q,T,H,M\geq 2$, $c_0>0$, and   $\{\mathcal{K}_i\}_{i\geq 1} $ be pairwise disjoint sets of characters $\chi (\mod q)$. For each $i$, define the  the multi-set
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_i=\{\lambda=(1-\beta)\log q: L(\beta+i\gamma ,\chi)=0, \lambda\leq H,|\gamma|\leq T,\chi\in \mathcal{K}_i  \},
 \end{equation}
and assume that     
 \begin{equation} 
 \max_{(\chi,\chi')\in \mathcal{K}_i^2}\operatorname{cond}(\bar \chi \chi')\leq M,\quad \min_{\lambda \in \mathcal{L}_i} \lambda\geq c_0.
 \end{equation} 
   Then for $q\geq q(T,H,M,c_0)$ we have  \begin{equation} \label{eq: final}
 \sum_{i\geq 1} \bb{\sum_{\lambda\in \mathcal{L}_i} e^{-\delta^{-1} \lambda}}^2 \leq 1-c_1,
 \end{equation} 
 where $\delta=0.291$ and $c_1>0$ depends only on $c_0$.
 \end{theorem}
 We will briefly introduce this reduction in the next section and then give a proof to the inequality \eqref{eq: final} in the rest sections. We achieve this improvement by introducing a dichotomy argument which can reduce nearly half of the number of zeroes under consideration. This will be shown in the end of Section 3.

