The proof of the main theorem uses Mukai's program for genus $11$: the birational map $\phi:\mathcal P\dashrightarrow \cM_{11}$ (Theorem~\ref{Mukai's program}) gives a “birational bridge between the moduli space of K3 surfaces and the moduli space of curves,” so that one can “transfer the problem to a problem about the moduli space of curves.” Concretely, the argument reduces to showing that $\operatorname{CH}^2(\cS_{11}')_\mathbb{Q}$ “can be controlled by” $\operatorname{CH}^2(\cF_{11}')_\mathbb{Q}$ together with $\operatorname{CH}^2(\overline{M}_{11,1})_\mathbb{Q}$. A second ingredient is the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}
\label{CH2 is tautologically generated for rationally connected moduli space introduction}
    Suppose $2g-2+n>0$, and the coarse moduli space $\ov{M}_{g,n}$ is rationally connected. Then $\CH^2(\ov{M}_{g,n})_\mathbb{Q}$ is generated by tautological classes.
\end{theorem}

Using \cite[Corollary 2]{Bar} that $\overline{M}_{11,1}$ is rationally connected, one gets that $\operatorname{CH}^2(\overline{M}_{11,1})_\mathbb{Q}$ is tautologically generated; this “complete description,” combined with Mukai’s bridge, allows us to conclude the proof of the main theorem.

